from gk_a import G,g,SCI,CH
BIO="Biology/Life Sciences"
CC="Chemical Composition & Classification"; CB="Chemical Bonding & Molecular Structure"; IA="Industrial, Materials & Applied Chemistry"; CR="Chemical Reactions, Stoichiometry & Energetics"
g(81,"Consider the following statements:\nI. A solution of iodine in ethanol known as 'tincture of iodine' is a heterogeneous mixture.\nII. A suspension is a heterogeneous mixture and its particles scatter a beam of light passing through it, making its path visible.\nIII. A colloidal solution, although it appears homogeneous, is a heterogeneous mixture.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?",["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"B",SCI,CH,CC,"Solutions, suspensions and colloids","3-Statement Combination","M","I is false: tincture of iodine is a true solution (homogeneous).\nII: suspensions are heterogeneous and their particles scatter light (true).\nIII: a colloid looks homogeneous but is heterogeneous (true).\nAnswer: (b)")
g(82,"Consider the following statements:\nI. A 30% solution of hydrogen peroxide is marketed as '10 volume' H₂O₂.\nII. In the compound copper sulphate pentahydrate, all the five water molecules are hydrogen bonded.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?",["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"D",SCI,CH,CB,"Hydrogen peroxide strength and hydrated copper sulphate","2-Statement Combination","H","I is false: a 30% (w/v) solution is marketed as '100 volume' H₂O₂; '10 volume' is about 3%.\nII is false: four water molecules are coordinated to Cu²⁺ and only the fifth is hydrogen bonded.\nAnswer: (d)")
g(83,"Consider the following statements:\nI. The leaves of nettle, a herbaceous plant, cause painful stings when touched accidentally because of the secretion of formic acid.\nII. When gypsum is heated at 100°C, it forms calcium carbonate hemihydrate, called Plaster of Paris.\nIII. When moistened with water, anhydrous copper sulphate forms a green-coloured copper sulphate dihydrate.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?",["I only","III only","II and III","I and II"],"A",SCI,CH,IA,"Everyday chemistry of formic acid, gypsum and copper sulphate","3-Statement Combination","M","I: nettle stings contain methanoic (formic) acid (true).\nII: heated gypsum gives calcium sulphate hemihydrate, not carbonate (false).\nIII: anhydrous copper sulphate turns blue as the pentahydrate forms (false).\nAnswer: (a)")
g(84,"Consider the following pairs:\nI. Fe(s) + CuSO₄(aq.) → FeSO₄(aq.) + Cu(s): Displacement reaction\nII. Na₂SO₄(aq.) + BaCl₂(aq.) → BaSO₄(s) + 2NaCl(aq.): Double displacement reaction\nIII. Fe₂O₃(s) + 3CO(g) → 2Fe(s) + 3CO₂(g): Redox reaction\nIV. 2KClO₃(s) → 2KCl(s) + 3O₂(g): Thermal decomposition reaction\nHow many of the pairs given above are correctly matched?",["One","Two","Three","All four"],"D",SCI,CH,CR,"Types of chemical reactions","Statement Count","E","I: iron displaces copper (displacement).\nII: ions exchange partners and BaSO₄ precipitates (double displacement).\nIII: CO is oxidised and Fe₂O₃ is reduced (redox).\nIV: KClO₃ decomposes on heating.\nAll four are correct.\nAnswer: (d)")
g(85,"What are the approximate percentages by mass of nitrogen and hydrogen respectively in urea?",["43% and 5%","47% and 5%","43% and 7%","47% and 7%"],"D",SCI,CH,CC,"Percentage composition of urea","Single MCQ","M","Urea is CO(NH₂)₂ with molar mass 60 g/mol.\nN: $\\frac{28}{60}=46.7\\%\\approx47\\%$. H: $\\frac{4}{60}=6.7\\%\\approx7\\%$.\nAnswer: (d)")
g(86,"Coconut oil upon hydrolysis with potassium hydroxide gives:",["Free fatty acids and sodium hydroxide","Glycerol and sodium salts of fatty acids","Glycol and potassium salts of fatty acids","Glycerol and potassium salts of fatty acids"],"D",SCI,CH,"Organic Chemistry & Carbon Compounds","Saponification of an oil","Single MCQ","Concept: oils and fats are triglycerides; alkaline hydrolysis (saponification) gives glycerol plus the salt of the fatty acids from the base used.\nWith KOH the salts are potassium salts.\nAnswer: (d)")
g(87,"Consider the following statements regarding hydrogen bonds:\nI. Hydrogen bonds can only form when hydrogen is bonded to oxygen, nitrogen or fluorine.\nII. Hydrogen bonds are intramolecular only.\nIII. The strength of a hydrogen bond decreases with a decrease in the electronegativity of the two bonded atoms.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are not correct?",["I only","II only","I and II","II and III"],"B",SCI,CH,CB,"Hydrogen bonding","3-Statement Combination","H","I is the standard textbook condition (H bonded to N, O or F), so it is taken as correct.\nII is wrong: hydrogen bonds are both intermolecular and intramolecular.\nIII is correct: lower electronegativity gives a weaker bond.\nOnly II is not correct.\nAnswer: (b)")
g(88,"Ionic compounds have high melting and boiling points. This is due to:",["the presence of strong electrostatic forces between ions in the crystal lattice.","the weak intermolecular van der Waals forces.","the presence of a 'sea of delocalized electrons'.","lower lattice energy."],"A",SCI,CH,CB,"Properties of ionic compounds","Single MCQ","E","Ionic solids are held by strong electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions in the lattice, and a large lattice energy must be overcome to melt them.\nAnswer: (a)")
g(89,"Which of the following statements is/are correct in respect of Portland cement?\nI. Adding more water (higher water-cement ratio) accelerates the initial setting time of cement.\nII. The addition of a small percentage of gypsum gives the desired retardation.\nIII. Gypsum is added during manufacturing to accelerate the setting time of cement.",["I and II","I and III","I only","II only"],"D",SCI,CH,IA,"Setting of Portland cement","3-Statement Combination","M","Gypsum is added to retard (slow) the setting, so II is true and III is false.\nA higher water-cement ratio delays, not accelerates, setting, so I is false.\nAnswer: (d)")
g(90,"Consider the following pairs:\nI. Cloud: Aerosol\nII. Mud: Sol\nIII. Milk: Emulsion\nIV. Rubber: Foam\nHow many of the pairs given above are correctly matched?",["One","Two","Three","All four"],"D",SCI,CH,CC,"Types of colloids","Statement Count","H","Cloud is a liquid-in-gas aerosol; mud is a solid-in-liquid sol; milk is a liquid-in-liquid emulsion; foam rubber is a solid foam (gas dispersed in a solid).\nAll four pairs are acceptable.\nAnswer: (d). Medium confidence: the rubber pair is the weak link.")
g(91,"Consider the following statements in relation to respiration in animals:\nI. Glucose breaks down into pyruvate in the cytoplasm of all cells in both aerobic and anaerobic conditions.\nII. The energy released during anaerobic respiration is greater than the energy released in aerobic respiration.\nIII. Carbon dioxide is released only in aerobic respiration.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?",["I only","II only","I and III","II and III"],"C",SCI,BIO,"Biomolecules, Enzymes & Bioenergetics","Aerobic and anaerobic respiration in animals","3-Statement Combination","M","I: glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm in both modes (true).\nII: anaerobic respiration releases far less energy (false).\nIII: in animals anaerobic respiration gives lactic acid and no CO₂, so CO₂ is released only in aerobic respiration (true).\nAnswer: (c)")
g(92,"In humans, the exit of partially digested food from the stomach into the small intestine is regulated by which one of the following structures/processes?",["Peristalsis","Muscular walls of stomach","Sphincter muscle","Villi in small intestine"],"C",SCI,BIO,"Human Anatomy & Systemic Physiology","Pyloric sphincter","Single MCQ","The pyloric sphincter at the stomach's lower end opens and closes to release food into the small intestine.\nAnswer: (c)")
g(93,"Which of the following cells form components of complex permanent tissue in plants?\nI. Companion cells\nII. Sieve tubes\nIII. Tracheids\nIV. Vessels",["I, II, III and IV","II, III and IV only","I, III and IV only","I and II only"],"A",SCI,BIO,"Plant Anatomy, Transport & Physiology","Complex permanent tissues","4-Statement Combination","Complex tissues are xylem (tracheids, vessels) and phloem (sieve tubes, companion cells).\nAll four are components.\nAnswer: (a)")
g(94,"Which one of the following is not a part of the hindbrain of human beings?",["Medulla","Pons","Corpus callosum","Cerebellum"],"C",SCI,BIO,"Human Anatomy & Systemic Physiology","Parts of the human brain","Single MCQ","The hindbrain has the cerebellum, pons and medulla oblongata.\nThe corpus callosum joins the two cerebral hemispheres (forebrain).\nAnswer: (c)")
g(95,"Which of the following organisms show external fertilization?\nI. Pigeon\nII. Frog\nIII. Fish\nIV. Lizard",["II, III and IV","II and III only","I and IV only","I, II and III"],"B",SCI,BIO,"Biological Classification & Diversity","Modes of fertilisation in vertebrates","4-Statement Combination","Frogs and most fishes shed gametes into water (external fertilization).\nPigeons and lizards (birds and reptiles) fertilise internally.\nAnswer: (b)")
g(96,"Which one of the following plant groups is characterized by having a haploid plant body?",["Gymnosperms","Angiosperms","Pteridophytes","Bryophytes"],"D",SCI,BIO,"Biological Classification & Diversity","Dominant generation in plant groups","Single MCQ","In bryophytes the dominant plant body is the haploid gametophyte.\nIn pteridophytes, gymnosperms and angiosperms the main body is the diploid sporophyte.\nAnswer: (d)")
g(97,"A flower typically is composed of:",["Stomata, Cotyledon, Petals, Sepals","Carpels, Sepals, Petals, Cambium","Carpels, Stamens, Petals, Sepals","Petals, Sepals, Perianth, Root cap"],"C",SCI,BIO,"Plant Anatomy, Transport & Physiology","Parts of a flower","Single MCQ","A typical flower has four whorls: sepals, petals, stamens and carpels.\nAnswer: (c)")
g(98,"Which one of the following organelles is found in both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?",["Nucleus","Mitochondria","Ribosome","Golgi complex"],"C",SCI,BIO,"Cytology & Cell Architecture","Organelles common to all cells","Single MCQ","Ribosomes occur in both prokaryotes (70S) and eukaryotes (80S).\nA nucleus, mitochondria and Golgi complex are absent in prokaryotes.\nAnswer: (c)")
g(99,"Which one of the following is not a connective tissue in the human body?",["Cardiac muscle","Cartilage","Blood","Bone"],"A",SCI,BIO,"Animal Histology & Cell Types","Types of animal tissue","Single MCQ","Cartilage, blood and bone are connective tissues.\nCardiac muscle is muscular tissue.\nAnswer: (a)")
g(100,"Which one of the following is not a characteristic of an insect-pollinated plant?",["Brightly coloured and fragrant flowers","Light and small pollen grains","Sticky and/or spiny pollen grains","Presence of nectar in a flower"],"B",SCI,BIO,"Ecology & Plant Adaptations","Pollination adaptations","Single MCQ","Insect-pollinated flowers are showy, scented and nectar-bearing with sticky or spiny pollen.\nLight, small pollen grains are a feature of wind pollination.\nAnswer: (b)")
if __name__=="__main__": print(len(G))
